{
  "artifact": "judging without the pixels: the reporting baseline, and the marking's blast radius",
  "version": 2,
  "note": "Two model-free measurements. The baseline reads every already-frozen image-condition row through a rule pinned in code before the rows were read through it, and asks whether a blind draft ever reported that the picture was unavailable. The blast radius drives the real drafter over every case of every scope `run_scope` defines, and counts where the system's own marking fires, what the contradiction guard would refuse, and what the report's trust label stops claiming — over the whole corpus rather than over the cases each was designed for. Version 2 adds the trust-label counts; every earlier number is unchanged, and re-derived rather than carried over.",
  "baseline": {
    "rule": "a drafted row signals that the visual evidence was unavailable iff its `remediation`, case-folded, contains any of the 13 pinned phrases in DETECTOR_PHRASES. Deliberately high-recall: the expected answer is zero, so the failure that would matter is a rule too narrow to catch a signal that was there, and a false positive costs one row read by eye and named in `signalling` below.",
    "phrases": [
      "cannot see",
      "can't see",
      "unable to see",
      "not able to see",
      "cannot view",
      "unable to view",
      "without seeing",
      "not shown the image",
      "no image was provided",
      "image is not provided",
      "image not provided",
      "no visual",
      "cannot verify the image"
    ],
    "field_read": "draft.remediation",
    "rows": 70,
    "blind_rows": 28,
    "blind_rows_signalling": 0,
    "sighted_rows": 42,
    "sighted_rows_signalling": 0,
    "conditions": [
      {
        "condition": "leaky/no-image",
        "attaches": "none",
        "blind": true,
        "rows": 7,
        "signalling": 0
      },
      {
        "condition": "opaque/no-image",
        "attaches": "none",
        "blind": true,
        "rows": 21,
        "signalling": 0
      },
      {
        "condition": "opaque/with-image",
        "attaches": "true",
        "blind": false,
        "rows": 21,
        "signalling": 0
      },
      {
        "condition": "opaque/mismatched-image",
        "attaches": "mismatched",
        "blind": false,
        "rows": 21,
        "signalling": 0
      }
    ],
    "signalling": [],
    "note": "The rule was written into code before these rows were read through it, and it reads the one field a drafted row has to carry the signal — `DraftRow` has no rationale. A blind row is one drafted under a condition that attaches no picture, taken from the frozen condition rather than from the row, so the denominator is the pre-registered set."
  },
  "blast_radius": {
    "scopes": [
      {
        "scope": "acceptance",
        "eval_set_id": "act-acceptance@1",
        "axe_rules": [
          "document-title",
          "empty-heading",
          "label",
          "link-name"
        ],
        "cases": 40,
        "findings": 54
      },
      {
        "scope": "image-leaky",
        "eval_set_id": "act-image-leaky@1",
        "axe_rules": [
          "image-alt"
        ],
        "cases": 7,
        "findings": 7
      },
      {
        "scope": "image-opaque",
        "eval_set_id": "act-image-opaque@1",
        "axe_rules": [
          "image-alt"
        ],
        "cases": 7,
        "findings": 7
      }
    ],
    "findings": 68,
    "pixel_decided": 14,
    "assembled_path": 0,
    "marking": {
      "visually_verified_false": 14,
      "visually_verified_true": 0,
      "visually_verified_none": 54,
      "note": "Counted as production drafts: no picture attached, no announcement. `True` is therefore not exercised by this sweep — it is the same predicate's other branch, reached wherever a picture is sent — and `None` is the answer for every class no picture decides, which is what keeps the marking from labelling the text classes unverified."
    },
    "contradiction_guard": {
      "degraded_as_shipped": 0,
      "degraded_when_claiming_seen": 14,
      "degraded_cases": [
        "1ff696703e7e7393a5d05cdcd3229cb050594998",
        "530266c6116fcfad12561e9e1a407fa0a0da3435",
        "607ad4964aa69e78a663cf993a28cedd6a1dc39e",
        "a2333ec76e676624212dcd616ed11ae576ab775e",
        "be6b29e220d6afbd827625c602ec49027e73fdf1",
        "cfd1636ab41c1418d1ad510eb9802c31fb2c5c5e",
        "f7406b89f8e6769c01da5c305e3e6c921fd7c1e4"
      ],
      "note": "Both sweeps hand the drafter the SAME answer, one claiming `seen` on every row, so the two counts differ in the ask alone. As shipped the guard is then structurally unreachable: `announce_image` defaults off, the response shape carries no field for the claim, and the claim is dropped rather than contradicted — a zero measured off the shape, not the absence of anything to catch. The second count is the adversarial upper bound, what flipping the announcement on would refuse against a model that claimed `seen` everywhere. Only a pixel-decided finding drafted without pixels is refused; every other row ships unchanged, because the announcement and the schema are both gated by class."
    },
    "trust_label": {
      "refused": "oracle-verified",
      "renders_as": "drafter-judged, no visual evidence",
      "counted_under": "every citation VERIFIED and no human review — the one state `oracle-verified` is reachable from, so the count is what the refusal actually costs rather than what it costs a row that was on the floor anyway",
      "rows_downgraded": 14,
      "rows_downgraded_correctly": 12,
      "rows_over_downgraded": 2,
      "over_downgraded_cases": [
        "a2333ec76e676624212dcd616ed11ae576ab775e"
      ],
      "text_decided_cases": [
        "a2333ec76e676624212dcd616ed11ae576ab775e"
      ],
      "rows_unmoved": 54,
      "note": "`PIXEL_DECIDED_RULES` is keyed by the RULE, so an instance of that rule which its own text decides — an `alt` that is a hex digest describes nothing whatever the pixels hold — is marked blind and downgraded too. That over-firing is expected and it is the conservative direction: the failure it prevents is a judgment made without the evidence it needed shipping under the report's strongest label, and the cost is a text-decidable row reading one label lower than it could. It is reported as a number rather than as a sentence, and the number is a FLOOR — the text-decided set is the one case the frozen permutation names, because that is the one anybody labelled. `Conformance` is untouched throughout: the row keeps the verdict the model gave, and only what the report claims stands behind it moves."
    },
